In article <HAIRSTON.90May23124508@henry.ece.cmu.edu> hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:
>[subbarao@phoenix.Princeton.EDU (Kartik Subbarao) writes:]
>[] Which brings up an interesting point. It would be interesting to see
>[] nethack expanded to represent the mythologies of more countries. What
>[] do you guys think?
>
>i'd enjoy hearing about other cultures/mythologies and how they might
>fit into nethack.

Well, the simplest thing would be to allow the player, if he wished,
to specify his own gods.  This would seem to be easy to implement; if
I were an Elric fan, I could just put into my NETHACKOPTIONS a line
like "gods:Donblas/Grome/Arioch", which would change nothing but the
names of the gods.  It wouldn't change play balance at all, and some
might get a kick out of it.  This ought to be easy to implement for
3.1, if the dev team likes it.

Also, since fruit on 'bones' levels stays the same, perhaps altars
ought to behave similarly.  e.g.  If I were playing a Archaeologist
(Aztec mythos), I might land on a Valkyrie's bones level and find "an
altar to Loki (chaotic)".  This would behave like any other chaotic
altar in terms of identifying blesses and curses, and could be
converted to my own god and alignment by a succesful sacrifice.  (A
less successful sacrifice might adjust the altar's alignment but not
it's theology; i.e. I might just change the altar to "an altar to Odin
(lawful).")  Praying at an altar not of my alignment would have the
same effects as usual; the only question would be, if I were (for
example) a lawful follower of Quetzlcoatl, what would be the result of
praying at a lawful altar of Odin?  Depending on luck, it could piss
off Quetzl as per usual, change my theology to Norse, give me some
surprise blessing (perhaps each god would have his own special
blessing; Odin might give me cold resistance, which might be hard to
acquire from Quetzl.), what-have-you.
